# winecoreaudio: Expose virtual endpoints for the default devices & automatic default device switching
# https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/merge_requests/11370 state=opened
# ## What problem does this solve?

Wine currently exposes the physical Core Audio device that happens to be the
macOS default when endpoints are enumerated. Windows applications therefore see
that physical device as the default endpoint. If the macOS default output is
changed while a stream is running, the application does not retain a persistent
"follow the system default" endpoint and Wine does not update all of the state
associated with the stream's new device.

This is visible when switching between outputs such as built-in speakers,
headphones, HDMI, or an external audio interface. A stream opened on the Windows
default endpoint should follow the new macOS default. Conversely, a stream
opened on a specifically selected physical device should remain associated with
that device and return to it after a disconnect/reconnect.

## What does this series change?

### 1. Expose persistent default endpoints

The first commit adds virtual `Default Output` and `Default Input` endpoints at
the start of the corresponding endpoint lists. Their device identifier is an
empty string, which is resolved through Core Audio's current default-device
property when Wine creates a stream or answers mix-format and format-support
queries. Physical endpoints continue to be identified by their Core Audio UID.

This separates the meaning of "use this physical device" from "use whichever
device is currently the system default". The virtual endpoint is only exposed
when at least one physical endpoint exists.

This mirrors a pattern already used by winepulse.drv: passing an empty/NULL
sink or source name to `pa_stream_connect_playback()`/`pa_stream_connect_record()`
requests PulseAudio's current default device, while `PA_STREAM_DONT_MOVE`
pins the stream to an explicitly named one
(`dlls/winepulse.drv/pulse.c:1088`-`1097`). winecoreaudio.drv's virtual
endpoint is the same idea applied to Core Audio, which has no equivalent
"use the default" argument at the stream-connect level, so it has to be

